**Supersedes:** [0001: duplicates share a subject line](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject).
That accepted record remains unchanged as history. This decision replaces its
subject-only rule for the KB's current triage procedure.

## Context

The [review of incident #4471](/kb/incidents/4471-review) finds distinct parser and
date-hint causes in a ticket described as a single known parser bug. Subjects and
intake tags do not establish which cause a report belongs to. The old decision
and its triage procedure were last reviewed in June 2024; their accepted status
does not make their assertions current.

## Decision

Merge reports only when the evidence supports a shared cause and compatible
affected product and path. First preserve and deduplicate report IDs. For the
date cases, use the existing `causeOf` and `sameCause` functions: resolve tenant
locale, tenant form version, and channel. Read the body for contradictions.

Do not substitute browser locale, guessed intake tags, matching subjects, or the
size of a ticket for that evidence. If tenant or cause is unknown, or the body
does not fit the model, keep the case for investigation rather than forcing a
merge. Revisit the model when evidence changes.

## Check the rule

<DedupeExplorer />

- App and email reports for `kettle.books` have the same parser cause despite
  different subjects.
- Form and app reports for `alder.physio` can have the same subject but different
  causes: booking-v3's hint versus the shared parser.
- Form reports for `copper.studio` and `morrow.tax` can have the same subject and
  channel but different causes because their form versions differ.
- Form reports for `pale.harbour` and `alder.physio` share the hint cause despite
  different subjects.
- Matching subjects from `unknown.sender` provide no known cause. Keep them
  unplaced until investigated.

## Consequences

Triage needs tenant lookup and evidence review instead of subject matching
alone. This costs attention but avoids hiding a second defect under the first
defect's fix. Retain the original report IDs and ticket links when splitting;
count each report once.

The desk can keep receiving and routing reports while engineering investigates
the separate causes. Historical classification is a starting point, not proof
that every report is fully explained. See the review's explicit anomalies,
ownership needs, and limits on production actions.

